{"op": "add", "path": "/candidates/4/mechanism", "value": "Under this view, the event log itself is merely an ordered sequence of facts; correctness under at-least-once delivery depends on the consumer's fold operation being commutative (or more precisely, idempotent with respect to duplicate events). The writer's only obligation is to append entries; the system tolerates duplicates because folding a set of events yields the same result regardless of insertion order or multiplicity, provided the fold operation suppresses duplicate effects (e.g., via deduplication keys or monotonic updates). The causal chain: writer appends event → log stores duplicate → consumer folds stream with duplicate-aware commutative operation → final state is invariant under reordering and duplication. This reframes the requirement from a property of the write operation (safety/idempotence) to a property of the read/aggregate operation (commutativity/idempotence of the fold)."}